      "notes": "\n<h2>Ciliated Cell</h2>\n\n<h3>What is a ciliated cell?</h3>\n\n<p>\nA <b>ciliated cell</b> is a specialised animal cell found lining parts\nof the respiratory tract.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What are cilia?</h3>\n\n<p>\nCilia are tiny hair-like structures on the surface of the cell.\nThey can beat in coordinated movements.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What do ciliated cells do?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe respiratory tract can trap dust and microorganisms in mucus.\nCilia move this mucus towards the throat, where it can be removed.\n</p>\n\n<h3>How is the cell adapted?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe cell has many cilia on its surface. Their coordinated movement\nallows mucus to be moved along the respiratory passage.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> Why are cilia important in the respiratory tract?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> Mucus traps particles and microorganisms.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> The mucus needs to be moved away from deeper parts\nof the respiratory system.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> Cilia beat and move the mucus towards the throat.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Cilia move mucus containing trapped particles and\nmicroorganisms towards the throat.</p>\n  ",
