      "notes": "\n<h2>Tissue</h2>\n\n<h3>What is a tissue?</h3>\n\n<p>\nA <b>tissue</b> is a group of similar cells working together to carry\nout a particular function.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why do tissues exist?</h3>\n\n<p>\nMulticellular organisms contain many specialised cells.\nCells with related structures and functions can work together as a tissue.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Examples</h3>\n\n<p>\nIn animals, <b>muscle tissue</b> contains muscle cells specialised for\ncontraction.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nIn plants, <b>xylem tissue</b> contains specialised structures adapted\nfor transporting water and mineral salts.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> A group of specialised muscle cells contracts\ntogether. What level of organisation do these cells form?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cells are specialised for contraction.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> They work together to perform the same general function.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> They form muscle tissue.</p>\n  ",
