      "notes": "\n<h2>DNA</h2>\n\n<h3>What is DNA?</h3>\n\n<p>\n<b>DNA</b>, or deoxyribonucleic acid, is the molecule that carries\ngenetic information in cells.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why is DNA important?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDNA contains instructions that influence how cells develop, function\nand produce proteins.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Where is DNA found?</h3>\n\n<p>\nIn eukaryotic cells, most DNA is found in the nucleus, organised into\nchromosomes.\n</p>\n\n<h3>DNA and cell division</h3>\n\n<p>\nBefore a cell divides, its DNA must be copied. This allows genetic\ninformation to be passed to the new cells.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> Why is DNA important when a cell divides?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> DNA carries genetic information.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> New cells need genetic information to function.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> Copying DNA before division allows the information to\nbe passed to the new cells.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA must be copied so that the new cells receive the\ngenetic information required for their functions.</p>\n  ",
