      "notes": "\n<h2>Metaphase</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens during metaphase?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring <b>metaphase</b>, duplicated chromosomes line up near the\nmiddle of the cell.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nSpindle fibres attach to structures associated with the chromosomes\nand prepare them for separation.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why is positioning important?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe chromosomes must be positioned so that their copies can be\nseparated towards opposite ends of the cell.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> How can you identify metaphase in a diagram of\nmitosis?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look at the chromosomes.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check whether they are arranged across the middle\nof the cell.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> If the duplicated chromosomes are lined up at the\ncell's equatorial region, the cell is in metaphase.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase is identified by duplicated chromosomes\nlined up near the middle of the cell.</p>\n  ",
