      "notes": "\n<h2>Anaphase</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens during anaphase?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring <b>anaphase</b>, the duplicated chromosome copies separate and\nmove towards opposite ends of the cell.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why is this important?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe separation ensures that the genetic material can be distributed\nbetween the two future nuclei.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What happens to chromosome movement?</h3>\n\n<p>\nSpindle fibres shorten or change their arrangement, pulling the\nseparated chromosome copies towards opposite poles of the cell.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has reached anaphase. What is happening to\nthe duplicated chromosome copies?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The chromosomes had previously lined up in the middle\nof the cell.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> Their duplicated copies now separate.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> The copies move towards opposite ends of the cell.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The duplicated chromosome copies separate and move\ntowards opposite poles of the cell.</p>\n  ",
