      "notes": "\n<h2>Telophase</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens during telophase?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring <b>telophase</b>, the separated chromosomes reach opposite\nends of the cell.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nNew nuclear envelopes form around the chromosome sets, producing\ntwo new nuclei.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThe chromosomes also begin to become less condensed.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why is telophase important?</h3>\n\n<p>\nTelophase re-establishes the nuclei that will contain the genetic\nmaterial of the new cells.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> How does telophase differ from anaphase?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> During anaphase, chromosome copies are being separated\nand moved apart.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> During telophase, the separated chromosomes have reached\nopposite ends.</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> New nuclear envelopes form around them.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Anaphase separates the chromosome copies, while\ntelophase forms new nuclei around the separated chromosome sets.</p>\n  ",
