      "notes": "\n<h2>Cytokinesis in Animal Cells</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens?</h3>\n<p>\nAfter the chromosomes have been separated and the nuclei begin to reform, the\nanimal cell membrane starts to constrict around the middle.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThis inward movement produces a <b>cleavage furrow</b>. The furrow becomes deeper\nuntil the cytoplasm is separated into two parts.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Why can the membrane pinch inward?</h3>\n<p>\nAn animal cell does not have a rigid cell wall. Its flexible cell membrane can\nchange shape and constrict around the middle.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has completed telophase. What visible change would you\nexpect next as cytokinesis occurs?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cell membrane begins to constrict.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> A cleavage furrow develops.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> The furrow deepens.</p>\n<p><b>Step 4:</b> Two separate daughter cells form.</p>\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n<p>\nThe cleavage furrow is the physical process that helps separate the cytoplasm\nof an animal cell.\n</p>\n  ",
