      "notes": "\n<h2>Metaphase I</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens in Metaphase I?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring Metaphase I, homologous chromosome pairs become arranged at the equator\nof the cell.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nSpindle fibres attach to the chromosomes and help position the homologous pairs\nfor separation.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What should you look for?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe important feature is that <b>homologous pairs</b> are positioned at the\nequator, rather than individual chromosomes being arranged independently.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows homologous chromosome pairs arranged across\nthe equator. Which stage is represented?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the position of the chromosomes.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> They are at the equator.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> They are arranged as homologous pairs.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase I.</p>\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n\n<p>\nMetaphase I positions homologous pairs so that they can be separated during\nAnaphase I.\n</p>\n  ",
