      "notes": "\n<h2>Telophase I</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens in Telophase I?</h3>\n\n<p>\nBy Telophase I, homologous chromosomes have reached opposite poles of the cell.\nThe cell then proceeds towards the second meiotic division.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nDepending on the organism and cell type, nuclear envelopes may reform before\nthe cell divides.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What has changed?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe homologous chromosomes have been separated into different regions of the\ncell. The chromosome number in each resulting nucleus is now reduced to one\nset.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> What major event has already occurred by the time a cell reaches\nTelophase I?</p>\n\n<p>\nHomologous chromosomes have separated and moved to opposite poles.\n</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Homologous chromosome separation has occurred.</p>\n\n<h3>What comes next?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe cell proceeds to <b>Meiosis II</b>, where sister chromatids will eventually\nbe separated.\n</p>\n  ",
