      "notes": "\n<h2>Prophase II</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens in Prophase II?</h3>\n\n<p>\nAfter Meiosis I, the cells enter the second meiotic division. During Prophase II,\nchromosomes condense and the spindle apparatus develops.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThe chromosomes are already present as one set because homologous chromosomes\nwere separated during Meiosis I.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What is different from Prophase I?</h3>\n\n<p>\nHomologous chromosomes do not pair again during Prophase II.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has already completed Meiosis I and is beginning its\nsecond division. Which stage comes first?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first meiotic division is complete.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second division begins with Prophase II.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Prophase II.</p>\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n\n<p>\nProphase II prepares the chromosomes for sister-chromatid separation.\n</p>\n  ",
