      "notes": "\n<h2>Metaphase II</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring Metaphase II, chromosomes line up at the equator of each cell.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nSpindle fibres attach to the chromosomes and position them so that sister\nchromatids can be separated during Anaphase II.\n</p>\n\n<h3>How is this different from Metaphase I?</h3>\n\n<p>\nIn Metaphase I, homologous chromosome pairs line up at the equator.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nIn Metaphase II, individual chromosomes line up at the equator.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> A chromosome is positioned at the equator of a cell during\nthe second meiotic division. Which stage is being observed?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> It is the second meiotic division.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> The chromosome is positioned at the equator.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> Chromosome alignment at the equator occurs during metaphase.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase II.</p>\n\n<h3>Key distinction</h3>\n\n<p>\nMetaphase I = homologous pairs at the equator.</p>\n<p>\nMetaphase II = chromosomes at the equator before sister chromatids separate.\n</p>\n  ",
