      "notes": "\n<h2>Telophase II</h2>\n\n<h3>What happens?</h3>\n\n<p>\nDuring Telophase II, the chromosomes reach opposite poles of each cell.\nNuclear envelopes can form around the chromosome sets.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThe chromosomes may then become less condensed as the cells move towards their\nfinal state.\n</p>\n\n<h3>What has been achieved?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe sister chromatids have been separated. Each new nucleus contains one set\nof chromosomes.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> What major event has already occurred by Telophase II?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis II has separated sister chromatids.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> The chromosomes have moved towards opposite poles.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> New nuclei can form around the chromosome sets.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Sister chromatids have been separated and the chromosome sets\nare at opposite poles.</p>\n\n<h3>What happens next?</h3>\n\n<p>\nCytokinesis separates the cytoplasm, leading to the formation of four cells.\n</p>\n  ",
