      "notes": "\n<h2>Sperm Cell Structure</h2>\n\n<h3>What is a sperm cell?</h3>\n\n<p>\nA sperm cell is a male gamete specialised for reaching and fertilising an egg.\nIts structure is closely related to this function.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Main structures</h3>\n\n<p><b>Head:</b> Contains the haploid nucleus.</p>\n\n<p><b>Acrosome:</b> A cap at the front of the head containing enzymes involved\nin interaction with the egg.</p>\n\n<p><b>Middle piece:</b> Contains many mitochondria that provide energy for movement.</p>\n\n<p><b>Flagellum:</b> Produces movement that helps the sperm swim.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a sperm cell have many mitochondria in its middle piece?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sperm must move.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> Movement requires energy.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitochondria release usable energy through respiration.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The mitochondria provide energy needed for movement.</p>\n\n<h3>Structure → function</h3>\n\n<p>\nFlagellum → movement.</p>\n<p>\nMitochondria → energy supply.</p>\n<p>\nHaploid nucleus → carries one set of chromosomes.</p>\n  ",
