      "notes": "\n<h2>Egg Cell Adaptations</h2>\n\n<h3>How is the egg specialised?</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe egg has features that support fertilisation and the early development of\nthe new organism.\n</p>\n\n<p><b>Large cytoplasm:</b> provides stored materials and cellular machinery.</p>\n\n<p><b>Haploid nucleus:</b> provides one set of chromosomes.</p>\n\n<p><b>Cell surface:</b> participates in interaction with sperm during fertilisation.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> Why is having a haploid nucleus important in the egg?</p>\n\n<p><b>Step 1:</b> The egg contributes one chromosome set.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2:</b> The sperm contributes another chromosome set.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3:</b> Their combination restores the diploid number.</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> A haploid egg allows fertilisation to restore the normal\ndiploid chromosome number.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n\n<p>\nThe egg is specialised not for movement, but for receiving sperm and supporting\nthe early stages after fertilisation.\n</p>\n  ",
