      "notes": "\n<h2>Sperm and Egg Comparison</h2>\n\n<h3>Both are gametes</h3>\n\n<p>\nSperm and egg cells are both haploid reproductive cells. Each contains one set\nof chromosomes.\n</p>\n\n<h3>But their structures differ</h3>\n\n<p><b>Sperm:</b> small, streamlined and adapted for movement.</p>\n\n<p><b>Egg:</b> large and contains substantial cytoplasm.</p>\n\n<p>\nThese differences reflect their different roles in fertilisation.\n</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\n<p><b>Question:</b> Why would it be incorrect to say that sperm and egg cells\nhave the same structure?</p>\n\n<p>\nThey perform different roles. Sperm must travel to the egg and deliver its\ngenetic material, while the egg receives the sperm and provides cytoplasm and\nresources for early development.\n</p>\n\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Their structures are adapted to different functions.</p>\n\n<h3>Quick comparison</h3>\n\n<p>\n<b>Sperm → movement + delivery of male genetic material.</b>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n<b>Egg → reception + resources for early development.</b>\n</p>\n  ",
