      "notes": "\n  <h2>Autotrophic and Heterotrophic Nutrition</h2>\n\n  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  The way an organism obtains organic nutrients is another important\n  characteristic used when comparing organisms.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Autotrophic nutrition</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  An organism with autotrophic nutrition makes organic food from simpler\n  inorganic substances.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  Green plants can manufacture glucose through photosynthesis using carbon\n  dioxide and water, with light energy absorbed by chlorophyll.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Heterotrophic nutrition</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Organisms with heterotrophic nutrition obtain organic nutrients from other\n  organisms or organic material.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  Animals obtain food by consuming other organisms or their products.\n  Fungi obtain nutrients by absorbing soluble substances from organic\n  material.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Why nutrition matters in classification</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Different groups of organisms obtain nutrients in fundamentally different\n  ways. This makes nutritional mode a useful characteristic when comparing\n  groups.\n  ",
