      "notes": "\n  <h2>Kingdom Animalia Compared with Kingdom Plantae</h2>\n\n  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Both plants and animals are multicellular eukaryotic organisms, but they\n  differ in important characteristics.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Cell walls</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Typical plant cells have cellulose cell walls.\n  Animal cells do not have cellulose cell walls.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Chloroplasts</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Photosynthetic plant cells may contain chloroplasts.\n  Animal cells do not contain chloroplasts.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Nutrition</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Most green plants are autotrophic and manufacture organic food by\n  photosynthesis.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  Animals are heterotrophic and obtain organic nutrients from other\n  organisms or organic material.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Movement</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Animals commonly possess specialised systems for movement.\n  Plants generally remain fixed in one place but show growth movements and\n  responses to stimuli.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Important point</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Both kingdoms contain multicellular eukaryotic organisms. Their differences\n  come from several characteristics, not simply from whether they move.\n  ",
