      "notes": "\n  <h2>Order to Family</h2>\n\n  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  A <b>family</b> is a taxonomic rank below order and above genus.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  A family contains one or more related genera.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Example</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Humans belong to the family <b>Hominidae</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  Hominidae includes humans and other great apes.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  This means humans can be traced through the hierarchy:\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  <b>\n  Animalia → Chordata → Mammalia → Primates → Hominidae\n  </b>\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Why family is useful</h3>\n\n  <p>\n  Family groups organisms that are more closely related than organisms merely\n  belonging to the same order.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n  Therefore, family is more specific than order.\n  ",
