      "notes": "\n<h2>Phagocytes and Lymphocytes</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>Phagocytes and lymphocytes are both types of white blood cells, but they perform different roles in the immune response.</p>\n\n<h3>Phagocytes</h3>\n\n<p>Phagocytes defend the body by engulfing and digesting pathogens and foreign particles.</p>\n\n<p>The main process is:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognise → engulf → digest.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Lymphocytes</h3>\n\n<p>Lymphocytes are involved in specific immune responses.</p>\n\n<p>Some lymphocytes produce antibodies that bind to specific antigens.</p>\n\n<p>Other lymphocytes contribute to immune regulation and destruction of infected or abnormal cells.</p>\n\n<h3>Why Both Are Important</h3>\n\n<p>The immune system uses several coordinated mechanisms rather than relying on one type of white blood cell.</p>\n\n<p>For example:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → antibodies bind → pathogen is targeted → phagocytes can engulf and digest it.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A bacterium enters the bloodstream.</p>\n\n<p>A lymphocyte produces antibodies that bind to antigens on the bacterium.</p>\n\n<p>A phagocyte then encounters the marked bacterium.</p>\n\n<p><strong>What can the phagocyte do?</strong></p>\n\n<p>It can engulf and digest the bacterium.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Distinction</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr>\n<th>Phagocytes</th>\n<th>Lymphocytes</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engulf and digest foreign material</td>\n<td>Specific immune responses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-specific engulfment</td>\n<td>Recognise specific antigens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Digest pathogens</td>\n<td>Some produce antibodies</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n  ",
