      "notes": "\n<h2>Photosynthesis</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> is the process by which green plants use light energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>\n\n<h3>Word Equation</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong></p>\n\n<p>Light energy and chlorophyll are required.</p>\n\n<h3>Balanced Symbol Equation</h3>\n\n<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>\n\n<p>The light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll and used to drive the reactions.</p>\n\n<h3>Where Does It Occur?</h3>\n\n<p>Photosynthesis occurs mainly in the chloroplasts of green plant cells.</p>\n\n<p>Leaves contain many chloroplasts, especially in the palisade mesophyll.</p>\n\n<h3>Why Is Glucose Produced?</h3>\n\n<p>Glucose is an energy-rich organic compound that can be used in respiration or converted into other substances needed by the plant.</p>\n\n<h3>What Happens to the Glucose?</h3>\n\n<p>Glucose can be:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>used in respiration;</li>\n<li>converted to starch for storage;</li>\n<li>used to make cellulose;</li>\n<li>combined with nitrogen-containing compounds to make amino acids and proteins.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A plant is placed in darkness for several hours and then tested for starch.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Why might little or no starch be detected in a leaf that was previously photosynthesising?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Without light, photosynthesis cannot continue normally, so new glucose is not produced and stored as starch.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Chain</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Light energy → chlorophyll absorbs energy → carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen.</strong></p>\n  ",
