      "notes": "\n<h2>Palisade Mesophyll and Photosynthesis</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>The <strong>palisade mesophyll</strong> is a layer of cells located beneath the upper epidermis in many leaves.</p>\n\n<h3>Many Chloroplasts</h3>\n\n<p>Palisade cells commonly contain many chloroplasts.</p>\n\n<p>This allows them to absorb substantial amounts of light energy.</p>\n\n<h3>Position</h3>\n\n<p>They are positioned relatively close to the upper surface of the leaf, where light intensity is often relatively high.</p>\n\n<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>\n\n<p>The palisade mesophyll is therefore a major site of photosynthesis.</p>\n\n<h3>Carbon Dioxide</h3>\n\n<p>Carbon dioxide entering through stomata diffuses through internal air spaces before reaching photosynthesising cells.</p>\n\n<h3>Water</h3>\n\n<p>Water is delivered to the leaf through xylem in the veins.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A palisade cell contains many chloroplasts and is located near the upper surface of a leaf.</p>\n\n<p><strong>How does each feature help photosynthesis?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Its position gives it good access to light, while its many chloroplasts provide substantial chlorophyll-containing structures for absorbing light energy.</p>\n\n<h3>Exam Rule</h3>\n\n<p>Structure marks should be linked directly to function.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Many chloroplasts → greater capacity to absorb light.</strong></p>\n  ",
