      "notes": "\n<h2>Plant Growth and Development</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>Plant growth and plant development are related but different processes.</p>\n\n<h3>Growth</h3>\n\n<p>Growth is a permanent increase in the size and dry mass of an organism or one of its parts.</p>\n\n<p>In plants, growth occurs mainly through cell division followed by cell enlargement and differentiation.</p>\n\n<h3>Cell Division</h3>\n\n<p>Cells produced by mitosis increase the number of cells in a growing region.</p>\n\n<h3>Cell Enlargement</h3>\n\n<p>Young plant cells can increase in size as they take in water and develop larger vacuoles.</p>\n\n<h3>Cell Differentiation</h3>\n\n<p>Cells can become specialised for particular functions.</p>\n\n<p>For example, cells in developing roots can become specialised into root hair cells.</p>\n\n<h3>Development</h3>\n\n<p>Development refers to the changes that occur as a plant becomes more mature and its structures and functions become increasingly specialised.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A seedling becomes taller and develops larger leaves.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Which process is being observed?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Growth is occurring because the plant is increasing in size.</p>\n\n<p>If cells are also becoming specialised into different tissues, development is occurring as well.</p>\n\n<h3>Important Distinction</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Growth = permanent increase in size or dry mass.</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Development = changes in structure, function and organisation as the plant matures.</strong></p>\n  ",
