      "notes": "\n<h2>Secondary Growth in Plants</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>Secondary growth is an increase in the thickness or girth of stems and roots.</p>\n\n<p>It is particularly important in woody plants.</p>\n\n<h3>Why Secondary Growth Matters</h3>\n\n<p>Increasing stem thickness provides structural support as a plant becomes larger.</p>\n\n<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>\n\n<p>The vascular cambium is a lateral meristem that produces new vascular tissues.</p>\n\n<p>It contributes to an increase in the thickness of the stem or root.</p>\n\n<h3>Primary vs Secondary Growth</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Primary growth → length.</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Secondary growth → thickness or girth.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A tree trunk becomes wider over several years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Which type of growth is mainly responsible?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Secondary growth.</p>\n\n<h3>Exam Trap</h3>\n\n<p>Do not confuse secondary growth with an increase in height.</p>\n\n<p>A stem becoming taller is primarily associated with primary growth.</p>\n\n<p>A stem becoming thicker is associated with secondary growth.</p>\n  ",
