      "notes": "\n<h2>Phototropism in Shoots</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>Phototropism is directional growth in response to light.</p>\n\n<p>Shoots generally show positive phototropism, meaning they grow toward a light source.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-Step Explanation</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Light comes mainly from one side.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Auxin becomes distributed unevenly in the shoot.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> More auxin accumulates on the shaded side of the shoot.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Cells on the shaded side elongate more.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> The shoot bends toward the light.</p>\n\n<h3>Why the Shoot Bends</h3>\n\n<p>The shoot does not bend because the light physically pushes it.</p>\n\n<p>It bends because the two sides grow at different rates.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A seedling is placed beside a window.</p>\n\n<p>Light enters mainly from the right.</p>\n\n<p>The shaded left side receives more auxin.</p>\n\n<p>The cells on the left elongate more.</p>\n\n<p>The shoot curves toward the right, where the light is coming from.</p>\n\n<h3>Exam Rule</h3>\n\n<p>Always explain phototropism as a difference in growth between two sides of the shoot.</p>\n  ",
