      "notes": "\n<h2>Plumule Emergence During Germination</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>The plumule is the embryonic shoot of a seed.</p>\n\n<p>As germination progresses, the plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>\n\n<h3>What the Plumule Produces</h3>\n\n<p>The developing shoot produces leaves and stem tissues.</p>\n\n<p>Once suitable leaves become photosynthetically active, the seedling can increasingly produce its own organic food.</p>\n\n<h3>Relationship with the Radicle</h3>\n\n<p>The radicle develops into the root system.</p>\n\n<p>The plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>\n\n<p>These two embryonic structures therefore establish the two major parts of the young plant.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>A germinating seed has produced a structure that grows upward and later develops leaves.</p>\n\n<p>This structure is associated with the plumule and developing shoot.</p>\n\n<h3>Important Distinction</h3>\n\n<p>The plumule is not the same as the mature shoot.</p>\n\n<p>It is the embryonic shoot region that develops into the shoot.</p>\n  ",
