      "notes": "\n<h2>Identifying Stages of Growth from a Graph</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>A plant growth graph can show periods when growth is fast, slow or temporarily unchanged.</p>\n\n<h3>Fast Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A steep gradient indicates a high growth rate.</p>\n\n<h3>Slow Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A less steep gradient indicates a lower growth rate.</p>\n\n<h3>No Measured Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A horizontal section indicates that the measured variable has not increased during that period.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>Suppose a shoot grows:</p>\n\n<p>Day 0–2: 1 cm → 3 cm</p>\n\n<p>Day 2–4: 3 cm → 7 cm</p>\n\n<p>Day 4–6: 7 cm → 8 cm</p>\n\n<p>Calculate each rate.</p>\n\n<p>Day 0–2:</p>\n\n<p>(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>Day 2–4:</p>\n\n<p>(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>Day 4–6:</p>\n\n<p>(8 − 7) ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>The plant therefore grew fastest between Days 2 and 4.</p>\n\n<h3>Exam Method</h3>\n\n<p>Never judge growth rate only from the final height.</p>\n\n<p>Compare the gradients of the relevant sections.</p>\n  \n<hr>\n\n<h2>Identifying Stages of Growth from a Graph</h2>\n\n<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>\n\n<p>A plant growth graph can show periods when growth is fast, slow or temporarily unchanged.</p>\n\n<h3>Fast Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A steep gradient indicates a high growth rate.</p>\n\n<h3>Slow Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A less steep gradient indicates a lower growth rate.</p>\n\n<h3>No Measured Growth</h3>\n\n<p>A horizontal section indicates that the measured variable has not increased during that period.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked Example</h3>\n\n<p>Suppose a shoot grows:</p>\n\n<p>Day 0–2: 1 cm → 3 cm</p>\n\n<p>Day 2–4: 3 cm → 7 cm</p>\n\n<p>Day 4–6: 7 cm → 8 cm</p>\n\n<p>Calculate each rate.</p>\n\n<p>Day 0–2:</p>\n\n<p>(3 − 1) ÷ 2 = 1 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>Day 2–4:</p>\n\n<p>(7 − 3) ÷ 2 = 2 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>Day 4–6:</p>\n\n<p>(8 − 7) ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm/day</p>\n\n<p>The plant therefore grew fastest between Days 2 and 4.</p>\n\n<h3>Exam Method</h3>\n\n<p>Never judge growth rate only from the final height.</p>\n\n<p>Compare the gradients of the relevant sections.</p>\n  ",
